        "text": "Dantidurga's Malwa expedition: after the death of his Chalukya overlord in 747 the Rashtrakuta Dantidurga marched north, extinguished the Gurjara kingdom of Nandipuri in southern Gujarat, and proclaimed the conquest of Malwa by performing the Hiranyagarbha-dana ceremony at its capital Ujjayini, where the defeated Gurjara king is said to have served him as door-keeper. The victory was not followed by annexation: Nagabhata I was still acknowledged as suzerain at Broach in 756 and left a kingdom in Malwa and Rajputana to his successors.",
        "belligerents": "Rashtrakuta Empire vs Gurjara-Pratihara Empire",
        "outcome": "Rashtrakuta victory and the submission of the Gurjara king, but no lasting conquest north of the Kim",
        "commanders": "Dantidurga — Nagabhata I",
        "lat": 23.18,
        "lng": 75.78,
        "coordApprox": true,
        "source": "The Age of Imperial Kanauj (HCIP IV), printed p. 20: \"The Rashtrakuta king Dantidurga, who reigned between c. A.D. 733 and 758, is said to have defeated the Gurjara king and made him serve as a door-keeper (pratihara) when he performed the Hiranyagarbha-dana ceremony at Ujjain\"; same page, \"it is more or less certain that his Gurjara adversary could not have been anybody else but Nagabhata I\" and \"it does not appear that Dantidurga's military victory was followed by any permanent conquest\". Printed p. 2 places the expedition after A.D. 747: \"Then he led an expedition into Malwa; and proclaimed its conquest by performing Hiranyagarbha-dana ceremony at its capital Ujjayini.\" Date approximate (c. 748-750). DISAGREEMENT ON THE RECORD: Wikipedia states flatly that Dantidurga defeated and subdued Nagabhata I in Gujarat and Malwa; HCIP IV reports the door-keeper episode only as a claim (\"is said to have\"), notes that the identification with the Pratihara rests on a pun on pratihara that \"is not accepted by all\" (printed p. 20), and holds at printed p. 21 that \"in spite of initial discomfiture Nagabhata was able to leave to his successors a powerful principality comprising Malwa and parts of Rajputana and Gujarat\"."
