      "originalSource": "The Age of Imperial Kanauj (HCIP IV), printed p. 26: \"This took place probably about A.D. 794-95, early in the reign of Govinda III, when the new king was engaged in the south in putting down the rebellion of his brother and fighting with the Ganga ruler. The brunt of the attack fell upon his viceroy of the north, Indra, who 'alone' is said to have defeated the Gurjara lord. But, as noted above, the Pratiharas also claimed victory, and probably gained some hill forts in Malwa. The northern frontier was, however, guarded effectively by Indra and his son Karkka, who says in one of his records that the Rashtrakuta king had 'caused his arm to become an excellent door-bar of the country of the lord of Gurjaras.'\" Printed p. 25: \"It may be concluded, therefore, that no party gained a decisive victory, though Nagabhata probably retained some of the captured hill-forts.\" Dated 794-795 in HCIP's own chronology, printed p. 508: \"794-795 Conflict between Nagabhata and the Rashtrakutas (p. 26)\". Coordinate approximate: a point on the Lata-Malwa frontier in the Narmada valley; the sources locate no battlefield. Distinct from Govinda III's own northern expedition of c. 800, already recorded. NOT IN WIKIPEDIA's Tripartite Struggle article, which mentions Indra only as securing the Vindhya passes for his brother. The year is placed in the first years of Nagabhata II as this atlas dates them (795-833, Wikipedia \"c. 795 - c. 833\"); the roll formerly opened in 800, a textbook rounding with no citation, which put these sourced campaigns before his accession.",
