        "note": "A feudatory Seuna. The Struggle for Empire (HCIP V), list of inscriptions p. 814: 'Bassein Inscription of Seunachandra II, S. 991' (= A.D. 1069). Sastri, A History of South India (block 166), names 'the distant Yadava ruler Seuna II of Devagiri' among the supporters of Vikramaditya VI in the Chalukya war of succession of the 1070s. HCIP V p. 172 records that Somesvara I's general Nagadeva put down a revolt of the Yadavas of Seuna-desa shortly before A.D. 1060. Dates approximate - the span is bracketed by the 1069 inscription and his son's record of A.D. 1100."
